WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.680
Two runs of the same workload, separated only by how memory is reached.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.560
On the left, the arrangement as it stands today.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.200
Eight processing cores behind a single data engine.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.040
That engine fetches one descriptor at a time, so seven cores wait while one is fed.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.120
Watch the utilization column on the left.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.960
It never clears 12%, and most of the cores sit at almost nothing for the whole run.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.120
On the right, the same silicon.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.160
The same multiply-accumulate count.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.440
The same 28-nanometer process.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.960
The same clock.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.120
The only change is that every core now owns its own load engine and pulls from banked static

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.200
memory in parallel.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.280
All eight climb to around 59% and hold there.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.960
Nothing has been added but the path to memory.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.960
The frame that costs 470 milliseconds today lands in about 85.
